#
# GitHub reads this file and offers a "Cite this repository" button; Zenodo reads
# it when it archives a release. Both matter for the same reason: a link to a
# GitHub repository or to a Streamlit URL is not a durable reference, and a tool
# that cannot be cited does not accumulate credit.
#
# The `doi` at the bottom is the Zenodo CONCEPT DOI: it resolves to the latest
# release, which is what belongs in a paper. Each release also gets a version
# DOI of its own, for citing the exact code someone ran - that one is on the
# Zenodo record, not here.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "VHH tool box"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata in this file."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Jonathan
    family-names: "Vaneyck"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3095-7420"
    affiliation: "Center for Protein Engineering (CIP), University of Liège, Belgium"
repository-code: "https://github.com/TyrosinDuck/vhh-analyzer"
url: "https://vhh-tools-app.org"
abstract: >-
  A workbench for single-domain antibodies (VHHs, nanobodies). It characterises
  sequences, annotates CDRs and frameworks in IMGT numbering, scores camelid
  hallmarks and humanness, flags chemical liabilities and aggregation-prone
  regions, groups panning output into clonal families, measures what a 3D model
  says rather than what the sequence suggests, reads VHHs out of Sanger
  chromatograms, diagnoses existing coding sequences for rare codons and GC
  stretches, and plans a restriction cloning between two plasmids. Any protein
  is accepted and classified; antibody-specific analyses are applied only where
  they mean something.
keywords:
  - nanobody
  - VHH
  - single-domain antibody
  - antibody engineering
  - developability
  - IMGT
  - phage display
  - codon optimisation
  - bioinformatics
license: MIT
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23742269"
